e position early.  \n- **Ng8-f6**: solid development, preparing castling and central control.\n\nAmong these, **Ng8-f6** is safe, consistent, and avoids premature pawn commitments. It also prevents White from gaining space with e2-e4 without challenge.\n\nReason:  \nNf6 is a highly effective developing move that prepares castling, supports central counterplay (…e5 or …d5), and avoids weakening my pawn structure prematurely. It’s a mainline opening move in many classical defenses (e.g., Pirc, Alekhine, or even King’s Indian setups). Since White has already developed a knight, I should do the same.\n\nAnswer: N: g8-f6'}
